      "name": "theme-base",
      "type": "registry:theme",
      "title": "JHAI Theme Base",
      "description": "The structural tokens this component set needs: a type scale, spacing rhythm, control heights, durations and measure caps, all prefixed --ui- so they cannot collide with your own. Colour is NOT here — components paint in shadcn semantics only, so your palette is already the palette. Install this first; every other @jhai item assumes it.",
      "author": "Justin Harris <justin@justinharris.ai>",
      "categories": [
        "theme",
        "tokens"
      ],
      "files": [
        {
          "path": "theme-base/theme-base.css",
          "type": "registry:file",
          "target": "src/styles/theme-base.css"
        }
      ],
      "docs": "Add ONE line to globals.css, immediately after the tailwind import:\n\n    @import \"tailwindcss\";\n    @import \"../styles/theme-base.css\";\n\nAdjust the relative path to wherever the file landed. There is nothing to rebrand here: every colour in every @jhai component resolves through the standard shadcn tokens, so editing your own --background / --foreground / --primary is the whole job.\n\nThe shadcn semantic defaults ship as cssVars rather than inside the CSS file, because the CLI merges cssVars into your project's own :root, which is declared later in globals.css than the @import. A mapping inside the file would render correctly on a bare project and silently lose on a real shadcn one.\n\nWHY type IS registry:theme AND NOT registry:base — measured, shadcn CLI 4.21.0. A registry:base item's cssVars block is silently ignored: the files land, the CLI reports success, and not one token reaches globals.css. registry:theme applies them correctly. No error, no warning, visible only by diffing globals.css after an install.",
      "meta": {
        "upstream": null,
        "upstreamLicense": null,
        "jhaiVersion": "1.0.0"
      },
